WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:03.002
 Tripp, Gene, congratulations.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.070
You fellas have made it
into the next evolution

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:05.371
of this competition.

00:00:05.471 --> 00:00:06.906
Now, we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:10.509
to recreate this.

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:11.510
GENE: Dang.

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:13.079
[laughter]

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:15.614
 Your final
challenge is to forge

00:00:15.681 --> 00:00:20.253
a Marine Corps officer's sword,
also known as a Mameluke.

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:21.854
You'll have four days
at your home forges

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.089
in which to complete
this challenge.

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:24.323
And at the end of
those four days,

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.593
you'll return and
present your swords

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.194
to our panel of expert judges.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.795
And after they've
thoroughly tested them,

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:33.198
they'll move one of you forward
to represent the Marine Corps

00:00:33.265 --> 00:00:35.635
at the Battle of the
Branches final competition

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:40.873
and give you that opportunity
to win a check for 50 grand.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.107
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:43.909
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:44.677
- Bro.
- Bro.

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:45.412
 Good luck.

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:50.549
GENE: So it's day one.

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.251
I'm back at my home forge
in Springfield, Virginia.

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.387
So I think the more
difficult aspects of the

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:57.089
build are the parameters
that I have to build it to.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.058
It's very specific
and very tight.

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:01.561
So we'll see what happens.

00:01:01.628 --> 00:01:04.831
Because the parameters are so
tight on the bend of the blade,

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:07.400
my crazy idea is to
take a 6-by-6 log.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.802
And then I'm going to
remove all the excess wood.

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:13.306
And it's going to leave me with
the blade shape that I want.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.173
Wow.

00:01:14.239 --> 00:01:15.808
That went better than I thought.

00:01:15.874 --> 00:01:20.112
I plan on heating up the metal
and whacking it on this log.

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:24.150
And it's going to bend
and conform to it.

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.953
I've seen a lot of the smiths
straighten out their blade

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.721
with a slap on the anvil.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:33.059
So why not try to do it on a big
old log and put an arch in it?

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:35.328
It seemed to work pretty well.

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:38.965
I, like, just jumped four hours
ahead of my whole schedule.

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:40.500
I'm getting ready
to heat treat it.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.869
I want to keep it thick because
I'm worried about a warp.

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:44.704
Quenching.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:47.474
[rock music playing]

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:51.911
I am exhausted.

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:53.846
The quench went great.

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:54.714
I'm ready to hit the rack.

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:57.783 line:7%
 All right.

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:59.285 line:7%
So it's day two.

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:01.387 line:0%
I'm getting ready to jump
right into some forging.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:02.889
Hammer time.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.725
Well, this is a very
thin, long blade.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.227
So what's difficult about
a thin and long blade

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.763
is just the pure fact
that it's going to warp.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:13.866
I'm constantly having to
hammer the big curves back out

00:02:13.932 --> 00:02:16.369
and try to keep it straight
as possible, all while trying

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:20.306
to draw it out and get the width
and the thickness that I want.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.074
It finally meets parameters.

00:02:22.174 --> 00:02:24.777
I'm finally able to
move on to quenching.

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:27.346
If I was to pick up a
crack, I'd start overtiming.

00:02:27.446 --> 00:02:30.650
Man, this thing can do
anything in the quench here.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.920
If this thing snaps, we're going
to be close to being screwed,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:34.754
man.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.326
I don't see any warps.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:44.697
It looks like it's
pretty straight.

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.332
Super stoked about that.

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:50.170
After today, I feel
pretty damn awesome.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:55.340
 Day 3, and I'm ready to
get going on sand casting

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.677
a guard for my Mameluke sword.

00:02:57.743 --> 00:03:00.179
I've never done any
stand casting before.

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:01.580
Go big or go home.

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:04.383
The consistency of the
sand is the big thing.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.286
I went with playbox sand,
mixed it with some kitty litter

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:10.022
and some water, got to
the right consistency,

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:13.226
and took a shot at it.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:16.261
I molded some clay in the
rough shape of a guard,

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:18.564
and I used that to
mold the sand around.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:22.068
I pulled out the clay, and now
I'm ready to heat up my brass.

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:24.837
Let's go cast some metal.

00:03:24.903 --> 00:03:26.138
My mold is ready.

00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:29.242
My brass is a big pool
of molten hot brass.

00:03:29.309 --> 00:03:31.677
I'm ready to pour it in,
so I'm going to go for it.

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:34.880
I'm really hoping this sand
cast goes well because I've

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:36.415
used a lot of time.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:39.985
And if it doesn't, I don't
know how I'm going to recover.

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:41.153
Moment of truth coming up.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.058
[rock music playing]

00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:46.626
Holy cow, it worked.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:47.760
I can't believe it.

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.094
I've just got to knock
the sand off, clean it

00:03:49.194 --> 00:03:51.664
up a little bit on the grinder.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:52.832
That's a guard.

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:53.600
We've got a guard.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.637 line:7%
TRIPP: Day 4, man.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:02.642
Today, I've got to sharpen this
blade and get it out of here.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:06.212
My Ka-Bar didn't do so well
in the rope-cutting test,

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:09.082
so I want this blade
to cut through whatever

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:11.351
those guys are going to
put this thing through.

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:14.286
I want that Marine Corps anvil
pretty damn bad just because I

00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:16.255
want to be able to
represent the Marines

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:17.589
in the Battle of the Branches.

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:19.725
I believe I can come
home with that 50 grand

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.327
and represent the
Marine Corps very well.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:24.397
[rock music playing]

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.934
Damn.

00:04:29.034 --> 00:04:30.803
[laughs]

00:04:33.171 --> 00:04:34.807 line:7%
 So this is the start of day 4.

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:36.843
So today, I've got to
start out with getting

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.145
it all fit together.

00:04:39.211 --> 00:04:41.380
When Doug Marcaida
swings this thing,

00:04:41.446 --> 00:04:45.751
it is going to slice
whatever it goes into.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:47.586
I realized I had a little
bit more time left,

00:04:47.653 --> 00:04:50.022
and I wanted to try to put
the names of my competitors

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:52.258
on the blade with some gun glue.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.459
Rick.
Garrett.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:53.959
Tripp.

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:54.927
Gene.

00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:55.895
I'm pretty happy with it.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:56.662
I know it's going to cut.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.431
I am done.

00:04:58.531 --> 00:04:59.799
DOUG: All right, Marines.

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:02.901
Welcome to the kill test.

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:05.138
To find out what lethal
damage your weapon will do,

00:05:05.204 --> 00:05:06.939
I'll take your weapon
and deliver killing

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.373
blows to this ballistics dummy.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:09.241
Tripp, you're up first.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:09.975
You ready for this?

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:10.742
 Get it, killer.

00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:11.544
 Hurrah.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.716
[rock music playing]

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:26.592
 Yikes.

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:38.837
 Whoa.

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:39.839
 Holy moly.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:45.711
 All right, Tripp.

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:47.714
First up, the
handle construction.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.181
It is comfortable.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.617
Your edge here is sharp.

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:53.986
Even cutting through the
skull, no issues at all.

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:54.720
No chipping.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:55.821
No blunting.

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:58.023
And when you thrust with
this, it penetrates.

00:05:58.123 --> 00:05:59.492
It cuts all the way out.

00:05:59.558 --> 00:06:02.861
Overall, sir, your
weapon will kill.

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:03.529
 All right.

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:04.496
Hurrah.

00:06:04.596 --> 00:06:05.864
 All right, Gene.
You're next.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:06.766
You ready?

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:09.168
 Let's do this.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:12.138
[rock music playing]

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:37.162
Oh, no.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:38.464
It bent it.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:42.501
DOUG: All right, Gene.

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:45.737
Your weapon picked up
a warp right here, OK?

00:06:45.837 --> 00:06:47.807
Your handle, coupled
with blood all over it,

00:06:47.873 --> 00:06:50.175
made it very slippery
and hard to hold

00:06:50.275 --> 00:06:52.545
onto because it's
blocky and smooth,

00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:54.880
and I lost my grip on it.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.916
That's a very dangerous
thing to happen when you're

00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:58.150
trying to wield the weapon.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.319
But your edge is still sharp.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:02.754
Your tip is still thrustable.

00:07:02.854 --> 00:07:05.490
And more importantly,
it will kill.

00:07:05.557 --> 00:07:07.092
 [laughs] That'll work.

00:07:07.192 --> 00:07:08.661
[laughs]

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:13.265
 All right, gentlemen.

00:07:13.331 --> 00:07:14.633
It's time for the strength test.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:16.202
So to test the overall
construction of your blade

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:17.636
and see if they're
going to hold an edge,

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:20.273
I'll be smashing
them into these pots.

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:22.241
Now, remember, this
test is not about what

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:25.677
your swords do to those pots,
but those pots to your swords.

00:07:25.744 --> 00:07:26.878
Tripp, you're up.
You ready?

00:07:26.945 --> 00:07:27.380
 Ready.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.016
DAVID: OK.

00:07:30.116 --> 00:07:32.919
[clanging]

00:07:35.454 --> 00:07:36.221
 Oh, my gosh.

00:07:36.288 --> 00:07:36.955
TRIPP: Yep.

00:07:37.022 --> 00:07:38.757
Holy moly.

00:07:38.824 --> 00:07:40.827
God, no.

00:07:44.863 --> 00:07:48.334
 Well, Tripp, a few things
before we get to the obvious.

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:50.703
Your handle construction--
very comfortable.

00:07:50.769 --> 00:07:51.871
It felt really good in my hand.

00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:53.839
It felt like I could
do a lot with it.

00:07:53.905 --> 00:07:58.810
But right there at the edge,
there's that little brown spot.

00:07:58.877 --> 00:08:00.279
You had a micro
crack on that edge.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:01.013
 Oh.

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:02.315
Yeah.

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:03.983
 So as soon as I hit
that pot, it cut loose.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.854
I loved its feel, its shape, and
its look, but obvious problem.

00:08:11.323 --> 00:08:12.624
 All right, Tripp.

00:08:12.691 --> 00:08:14.092
While I know any Marine
could take that blade

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:16.828
and still continue to
fight, in this competition,

00:08:16.928 --> 00:08:19.065
your blade suffered a
catastrophic failure

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:20.966
after one strike on that base.

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.235
Gene, your blade
only has to survive

00:08:23.335 --> 00:08:24.904
one strike on these pots.

00:08:27.206 --> 00:08:30.108
 The entire Marine championship
for "Forged in Fire"

00:08:30.208 --> 00:08:31.744
lies on one swing.

00:08:31.811 --> 00:08:35.248
And I've got a big old warp in
my blade, so it's not over yet.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:38.184
[rock music playing]

00:08:43.288 --> 00:08:44.724
[thud]

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:47.293
 Nice job.

00:08:50.763 --> 00:08:53.565
 Gene, first off,
you're in one piece.

00:08:53.666 --> 00:08:55.801
You picked up that
bend earlier on,

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:58.103
but you didn't pick up
anymore in this test.

00:08:58.203 --> 00:08:59.338
Strong blade.
Held up.

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:00.071
Good job.

00:09:00.138 --> 00:09:00.873
 Thank you.

00:09:04.242 --> 00:09:05.244
 All right, Tripp.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:07.179
Looks like Gene's blade held up.

00:09:07.279 --> 00:09:10.449
That means that you cannot move
forward to represent the Marine

00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:12.217
Corps in our next challenge.

00:09:12.284 --> 00:09:13.686
Please leave the forge.

00:09:13.786 --> 00:09:14.953
 Super disappointed.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.655 line:0%
There's really no
way to tell that you

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:18.657 line:0%
have, like, any minor cracks
or anything like that.

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.826 line:0%
So you just-- hey, you find
out when you test them.

00:09:20.926 --> 00:09:22.360
That's how we found out today.

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:23.629
Get it, brother.

00:09:23.696 --> 00:09:25.030
Gene's going to
freaking bring home

00:09:25.097 --> 00:09:27.466
that Battle of the Branches
Marine Corps anvil.

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:28.901
He's winning it for
the Marine Corps.

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:33.739
 Gene, congratulations.

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:35.241
You've won this competition.

00:09:35.307 --> 00:09:37.510
And that means that you're
going to be moving forward

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:40.346
into our Battle of the
Branches final competition

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:43.382
to represent the Marine Corps
at the Marine Corps anvil.

00:09:43.482 --> 00:09:44.883
And that's going to
give you an opportunity

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:47.219
to win a check for $50,000.

00:09:47.285 --> 00:09:47.986
Good job, brother.

00:09:48.053 --> 00:09:48.854
 Thank you.

00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:50.422
[applause]

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:51.323
Holy cow.

00:09:51.423 --> 00:09:52.658
I did not see this coming.

00:09:52.725 --> 00:09:54.526
It's been a fun
ride up until now.

00:09:54.626 --> 00:09:56.629
But now, I've got to
represent the Marines.

00:09:56.696 --> 00:09:59.764
So now, it's game on, and
it's about to get real.

00:09:59.865 --> 00:10:01.967
Those other branch service
members better look out.

00:10:02.034 --> 00:10:03.836
[yelling]

